WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:02.070
Instructor: The aliases we've written so far

00:02.070 --> 00:03.480
have been simple.

00:03.480 --> 00:05.430
There's, what, three of them,

00:05.430 --> 00:07.260
and we have not worked with any arguments.

00:07.260 --> 00:09.720
We haven't passed any additional values to them.

00:09.720 --> 00:12.030
For example, when we run git branch,

00:12.030 --> 00:14.340
often we'll create a new branch

00:14.340 --> 00:16.560
instead of just looking at all branches.

00:16.560 --> 00:18.300
If we do git branch and then a branch name,

00:18.300 --> 00:19.830
it creates the branch.

00:19.830 --> 00:21.690
That would be an argument of value

00:21.690 --> 00:23.940
we're passing to the command.

00:23.940 --> 00:26.070
Or git commit.

00:26.070 --> 00:29.610
If we do dash M, we provide a commit message.

00:29.610 --> 00:31.800
So how can I set up an alias?

00:31.800 --> 00:34.860
For example, let's say I just want C

00:34.860 --> 00:38.190
or maybe CM for commit with a message.

00:38.190 --> 00:40.890
So I could write that alias myself.

00:40.890 --> 00:43.980
CM equals commit dash M.

00:43.980 --> 00:47.010
But then, what about the commit message itself?

00:47.010 --> 00:50.130
Well, the way that Git aliases are set up,

00:50.130 --> 00:51.120
the way they're structured,

00:51.120 --> 00:53.910
is that Git will actually take any arguments

00:53.910 --> 00:57.570
we pass to whatever our alias is, this command.

00:57.570 --> 01:02.190
If we pass something like first commit, for example,

01:02.190 --> 01:05.100
it will take that and pass it through here.

01:05.100 --> 01:07.560
So it will just append it to the end.

01:07.560 --> 01:10.293
So if we have two arguments that we pass through,

01:11.340 --> 01:13.230
for whatever reason, it wouldn't make sense here,

01:13.230 --> 01:15.840
but if we did, Git would pass both of them

01:15.840 --> 01:17.310
in that same order.

01:17.310 --> 01:19.170
So this makes it really really easy

01:19.170 --> 01:22.080
to define aliases that involve arguments.

01:22.080 --> 01:24.270
We can do a lot more complex things.

01:24.270 --> 01:28.470
For example, CM, if we want that for commit message.

01:28.470 --> 01:31.830
So I wouldn't just do C, because a lot of the time,

01:31.830 --> 01:33.655
you need to write full commit messages,

01:33.655 --> 01:35.941
open it up in the editor, multiple lines,

01:35.941 --> 01:39.300
follow a particular format, template for your message,

01:39.300 --> 01:44.190
so I would do CM as the shortcut for dash M.

01:44.190 --> 01:47.100
So if I just do that and I save,

01:47.100 --> 01:48.660
do I have any changes in here?

01:48.660 --> 01:51.543
Let's do our git l, or rather, s.

01:53.040 --> 01:54.540
I don't have anything to commit,

01:54.540 --> 01:56.610
so let's just make a new file.

01:56.610 --> 02:01.610
Let's do touch new.js, all right, and then git s again.

02:02.880 --> 02:07.020
We can add new.js. We didn't set up an alias for that.

02:07.020 --> 02:10.230
And now I can do get cm and then a message,

02:10.230 --> 02:12.873
like my new commit.

02:13.710 --> 02:15.060
All right.

02:15.060 --> 02:17.118
Oh, I totally forgot not to use exclamation points

02:17.118 --> 02:18.870
in the terminal.

02:18.870 --> 02:22.140
And if we take a look, get l for get log,

02:22.140 --> 02:24.330
we see my new commit.

02:24.330 --> 02:28.920
So we successfully made a new commit using CM.

02:28.920 --> 02:32.640
So we could do another example, git add.

02:32.640 --> 02:34.170
There's another one, right?

02:34.170 --> 02:35.460
We add files.

02:35.460 --> 02:38.820
We could just do A equals add,

02:38.820 --> 02:41.580
and I can add as many arguments after,

02:41.580 --> 02:43.920
you know, file one, file two,

02:43.920 --> 02:45.540
they all will be included.

02:45.540 --> 02:47.040
They'll be passed through.

02:47.040 --> 02:50.940
When I do git a, they'll be passed through to git add.

02:50.940 --> 02:52.380
So let's try that.

02:52.380 --> 02:55.500
Here is the repository I'm working on.

02:55.500 --> 02:58.860
Let's just add some changes to this README file

02:58.860 --> 03:02.940
and to this JS file. and to our package.json.

03:02.940 --> 03:04.503
I'm really destroying things.

03:05.490 --> 03:06.780
What should we change in here?

03:06.780 --> 03:08.640
Maybe a version number.

03:08.640 --> 03:11.640
It doesn't matter, I'm just making a lot of changes.

03:11.640 --> 03:12.473
Okay?

03:12.473 --> 03:14.760
So if I type git s now,

03:14.760 --> 03:17.730
instead of status, why not use our new alias?

03:17.730 --> 03:21.180
If I want to add two of them, the README and new.js,

03:21.180 --> 03:25.710
I can do git a, and then README and new.js.

03:25.710 --> 03:27.510
Now, this only saved us two characters, right?

03:27.510 --> 03:29.880
Add is not that long of a command.

03:29.880 --> 03:31.650
None of these are saving us a lot,

03:31.650 --> 03:35.010
although, you know, it's annoying to do git commit -m.

03:35.010 --> 03:36.900
But I'm gonna show you in the next video

03:36.900 --> 03:39.300
some more complicated commands or aliases

03:39.300 --> 03:42.210
that people like to use that we'll just steal.

03:42.210 --> 03:45.153
Well, they're shared for us to use, so we will borrow.

03:45.990 --> 03:47.590
But lemme just demonstrate this.

03:48.600 --> 03:51.150
So git a with these two arguments,

03:51.150 --> 03:54.690
they both were passed through, those two values, to git add.

03:54.690 --> 03:56.580
And if we take a look at our status,

03:56.580 --> 03:58.320
both of them are ready to be committed,

03:58.320 --> 04:00.693
and now we can do commit with a message.

04:01.620 --> 04:04.050
You know, another commit.

04:04.050 --> 04:05.430
Yay.

04:05.430 --> 04:08.820
And we'll do git l, and there we are.

04:08.820 --> 04:10.980
Another commit, yay.

04:10.980 --> 04:13.560
Okay, so you don't really have to worry about arguments.

04:13.560 --> 04:15.480
You don't have to do anything fancy.

04:15.480 --> 04:17.190
They're automatically passed through

04:17.190 --> 04:19.353
when you define an alias and call it.
